"Heinz J . Mauelshagen" <mauelshagen@sistina.com> writes:

> dd if=/dev/md0 of=urs.mdo.vgda.vorher bs=1k count=512  # Gut-VGDA
> dd if=/dev/md0 of=urs.mdo.vgda.nachher bs=1k count=512 # Schlecht-VGDA
> 
> reicht mir aus.

Hi Heinz,

as I described to you at the Linux-Kongress in Enschede, the lvreduce
tool, version 1.0, from the debian woody distribution corrupts the
metadata, causing vgscan to fail on the next boot.  The kernel is
Linux-2.4.12-ac1 with LVM-1.0.1rc4 patched into it.

Here is what we tried to do: We have LVM with one VG vg0 on a RAID-1
device /dev/md0 and have all file systems (including /) on LVs except
a small /boot with the kernel image and initrd image.  Our
/dev/vg0/news is currently 14.65 GB large (3750 PEs of 4 MB each) and
we wanted to reduce that to 4 GB (1024 PEs).  After the lvreduce, the
VGDA is corrupt.  We tried 2 times and it's reproducable.  We did
restore the previous metadata using vgcfgrestore.

Today, I tried a third time producing some debug output.  I did (while
/dev/vg0/news was mounted, i.e. the LV was opened):

    $ tune2fs -l /dev/vg0/root >& tune2fs.log
    $ dd if=/dev/md0 of=dev.md0.vgda.before bs=1k count=512
    $ lvdisplay -v /dev/vg0/news >lvdisplay.before
    $ lvreduce -vdf /dev/vg0/news -L4G >& lvreduce.log
    $ dd if=/dev/md0 of=dev.md0.vgda.after bs=1k count=512
    $ lvdisplay -v /dev/vg0/news >lvdisplay.after

After this, the metadata are corrupt, as can be seen in the output
file lvdisplay.after.  It shows

   --- Distribution of logical volume on 1 physical volume  ---
   PV Name                  PE on PV     reads      writes
   /dev/md0                 1025         203        79       
                            ^^^^
                             |
                             +---  wrong PE count


while the correct value should be 1024, but the mapping table
correctly shows 1024 entries:

   --- Logical extents ---
   LE    PV                        PE     reads      writes
   00000 /dev/md0                  07061  33         8        
   00001 /dev/md0                  07062  27         0      
   ...
   01022 /dev/md0                  08083  0          0        
   01023 /dev/md0                  08084  0          0        


Also, the output of lvreduce looks like all PEs were correctly released:

    $ grep release lvreduce.log
    <22> pv_release_pe -- CALLED
    <22> pv_release_pe -- pv_name: /dev/md0  pe: 10810  sector: 88555896
    <22> pv_release_pe -- pv_name: /dev/md0  pe: 10809  sector: 88547704
    <22> pv_release_pe -- pv_name: /dev/md0  pe: 10808  sector: 88539512
    <22> pv_release_pe -- pv_name: /dev/md0  pe: 10807  sector: 88531320
    ...
    <22> pv_release_pe -- pv_name: /dev/md0  pe: 8087  sector: 66249080
    <22> pv_release_pe -- pv_name: /dev/md0  pe: 8086  sector: 66240888
    <22> pv_release_pe -- pv_name: /dev/md0  pe: 8085  sector: 66232696
    <22> pv_release_pe -- LEAVING with ret: 0
    <1> lv_setup_for_reduce -- AFTER pv_release_pe


I have tarred and gzipped all the files and appended the tar file to
this mail.



Unfortunately, I can not restore the metadata using vgcfgrestore
currently, while the machine is running, since the VG is active, and
since / is on it, I can't deactivate it.  Is there another way to
restore the metadata except to do it from a rescue disk or initrd,
i.e. without rebooting?  This is a server (WWW, FTP, DNS, News, ...)
so I'd like not having to reboot.


urs





